I wonder whether it matters to have highly correlated random effects. I seem
to have read that these may cause problems. If they do, how to remedy this? 

 

My understanding is that there may be concerns about high correlations among
random effects as sometimes being a symptom of uncertainty in the estimate
of the random effects covariance matrix, which the z statistic wont
reflect.  In general, however, large positive or negative correlations among
random effects can reflect structure in ones data and I would certainly
recommend against taking out random correlations on the basis that theyre
close to ±1 when theyre included in the model!

 

Id suggest that you confirm your fixed-effects inferences with the
likelihood ratio test, which should be more robust to these kinds of issues.





Also, is it OK to get rid of random slopes with near-zero variances (sounds
anti-Barr though).

 

Well, OK is a relative term: the smaller the true variance of the random
slope for predictor X, the less anti-conservative inferences about the fixed
effect of X will be if the random slope is omitted.

 

Keep in mind, however, that what counts as small variance depends on the
scale of X.  If youre using sum coding for Group and Accent, then none of
your variances count as near-zero.  Id want to see a standard deviation
well below 0.1 before I started thinking about it as potentially near-zero
for a mixed logit model where the predictors are sum-coded.

 

