Hi Julia,


On Mar 13, 2015, at 2:50 PM, Julia Strand <jstrand-dmoCqaWXHRX2fBVCVOL8/A at public.gmane.org> wrote:

> I'd like to follow up to Roger Levy's response with another correlated random effects question.
> 
> I want to test whether two methods of calculating lexical competition (method1 and method2) each predict unique variance in word recognition accuracy. The two methods are highly correlated, but are different in theoretically interesting ways. 
> 
> I can generate models that include one method each and then a model that includes both methods and use a likelihood test to evaluate whether the model with both is a better fit than the models with only one, as below (omitting random slopes here for simplicity)
> 
> m1 = glmer (accuracy ~  method1 + (1|participants) + (1|items), data, family = binomial)
> m2 = glmer (accuracy ~  method2 + (1|participants) + (1|items), data, family = binomial)
> m1.2 = glmer (accuracy ~  method1 + method2 + (1|participants) + (1|items), data, family = binomial)

(A note for below: I’m going to use x1, x2, and y in lieu of method1, method2, and y for succinctness.)

> anova(m1, m1.2)
> anova(m2, m1.2)
> 
> The anovas tell me whether m1.2 provides a better fit than m1 or m2 alone, but I'm also interested in the direction of the effects when both are in the model (that is, are method1 and method2 pulling in the same direction?). However, my understanding is that I can't trust the signs of the estimates in m1.2, given the degree of collinearity. 

Just to be clear, this isn’t a question about correlated random effects, but rather a question about correlated predictors and how to interpret their slopes in a model that contains both of them.

> How can I determine the direction of the relationship between two collinear variables and the dependent measure? I'm aware of the residualizing technique to make the two methods orthogonal before entering them, but Wurm & Fisicaro have raised good objections to that method.

To answer your query, I’m going to pose a counter-query: what question is it that you want your regression model to answer?  In your model m1.2, the interpretation of the regression coefficient for x1 is, what is the effect on y as the value of x1 changes while the value of x2 is held constant?  (And likewise for x2, mutatis mutandis.)  As Wurm & Fisicaro point out, replacing x1 with a residual from a regression on x2 (e.g., creating x1.res <- resid(lm(x1 ~ x2)) and then using the formula y ~ x1.res + x2) does not change the interpretation of the regression weight for x1, though it *does* change the interpretation of the regression weight for x2.

So, if the regression coefficients in m1.2 don’t have the interpretation that you’re looking for, what interpretation *are* you looking for?

Best & I hope this helps!

Roger

