Hi Julia,

On Mar 18, 2015, at 8:36 AM, Julia Strand <jstrand-dmoCqaWXHRX2fBVCVOL8/A at public.gmane.org> wrote:

> Hi Roger,
> 
> Thanks for your reply!
> 
> You've pointed out exactly the question I want to ask - for x1, what is the effect on y as the value of x1 changes while the value of x2 is held constant? My concern was that putting highly correlated predictors into the model simultaneously makes the regression coefficients become "unstable" and can no longer be used to answer that question. Is this not the case?

This is certainly a possible outcome, but if it happens it is reflected in a large standard error of the parameter estimate.  This is exactly what multiple regression is intended to address.

> I'm including sample output below - as you can see, the sign for method1 changes when put in the model simultaneously with method2.

It’s not unusual to find cases when the sign of the weight for x1 differs in models y ~ x1 and y ~ x1 + x2.  e.g., when x1 actually mildly reduces y but is correlated with x2, which has a stronger effect of increasing y.

> This is exactly what I'd predict, based on how the methods were calculated (the hypothesis is that words will have highest accuracy when they are low on method1 and high on method2). If the signs of the estimates can still be trusted when correlated predictors are entered, then m1.2 is all I need.

The signs of the estimates can still be trusted in the sense that *if your model is an accurate specification of the structure of the generative process underlying the data*, the confidence levels given by the regression are what they say they are.

All that being said, this small set of regression models alone will not tell you whether your model is accurately specified (or near enough to being properly specified to give results that can be interpreted at face value).  Assessing accuracy of model specification is a much more subtle and open-ended problem — for example, your data could underlyingly require non-linear effects of your predictors, may have clustering structure that is not captured in your random-effects specification, and so forth.

> Exactly as you (and Wurm) say, if I use the residualizing method to create method2.resid as you describe and enter it in the model with method1, the result is the same (positive slope for method2, negative slope for method1). However, if I can just use the output of m1.2 to answer my question, that is a much simpler way of making the same point, without dragging the issues associated with residualizing in.

I don’t think you’ve brought up any issues that suggest you’re gaining anything from residualization!

Best & hope this helps,

Roger

